WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:06.697
Here is a formula: n squared, plus n

2
00:00:06.697 --> 00:00:08.339
plus forty-one.

3
00:00:08.339 --> 00:00:12.136
At n equals zero it gives forty-one.

4
00:00:12.136 --> 00:00:16.651
Then forty-three, forty-seven, fifty-three

5
00:00:16.651 --> 00:00:17.780
sixty-one.

6
00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:20.279
Every one of them is prime.

7
00:00:20.359 --> 00:00:22.508
A computer checks the first

8
00:00:22.587 --> 00:00:26.010
forty values of n, and all forty are prime.

9
00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.264
So is it prime for every n?

10
00:00:29.264 --> 00:00:31.200
Pause and decide.

11
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:36.540
Try n equals forty.

12
00:00:36.633 --> 00:00:39.134
Forty squared plus forty is

13
00:00:39.226 --> 00:00:41.264
forty times forty-one.

14
00:00:41.264 --> 00:00:43.487
Add one more forty-one

15
00:00:43.487 --> 00:00:47.100
and you get forty-one times forty-one.

16
00:00:47.450 --> 00:00:48.528
Not prime.

17
00:00:48.528 --> 00:00:51.762
The pattern held forty times

18
00:00:51.762 --> 00:00:53.810
and then it broke.

19
00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.358
Checking cases, however many

20
00:00:56.358 --> 00:00:59.389
never proves a statement about every n.

21
00:00:59.389 --> 00:01:02.800
There are always more cases than we checked.

22
00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.879
Here is another pattern.

23
00:01:05.879 --> 00:01:08.378
Add up the odd numbers.

24
00:01:08.378 --> 00:01:10.460
One is one squared.

25
00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:15.184
One plus three is four, two squared.

26
00:01:15.184 --> 00:01:19.315
Plus five is nine, three squared.

27
00:01:19.315 --> 00:01:23.810
Plus seven is sixteen, four squared.

28
00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:27.202
Each new odd number wraps one more

29
00:01:27.291 --> 00:01:29.438
layer around the square.

30
00:01:29.528 --> 00:01:32.122
Is the sum of the first n odd

31
00:01:32.211 --> 00:01:34.448
numbers always n squared?

32
00:01:34.448 --> 00:01:37.400
We need a proof, not more cases.

33
00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:40.046
Picture a row of dominoes

34
00:01:40.046 --> 00:01:42.351
one for each natural number.

35
00:01:42.351 --> 00:01:44.258
If the first one falls

36
00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:46.801
and every falling domino knocks

37
00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.900
over the next one, then they all fall.

38
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:53.547
That is the principle of induction.

39
00:01:53.547 --> 00:01:57.691
Two facts: a base case, P of c, and a step

40
00:01:57.691 --> 00:02:01.930
P of k implies P of k plus one, for every k.

41
00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:04.398
Neither fact works alone.

42
00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:07.025
A domino too far from the next

43
00:02:07.109 --> 00:02:08.804
one stops the chain.

44
00:02:08.889 --> 00:02:11.346
And a perfect row that nobody

45
00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:13.040
pushes never moves.

46
00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.091
Now prove the odd number pattern.

47
00:02:17.091 --> 00:02:20.456
Call it P of n: one plus three

48
00:02:20.456 --> 00:02:23.927
and so on up to two n minus one

49
00:02:23.927 --> 00:02:25.820
equals n squared.

50
00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:28.716
Step one, the base case.

51
00:02:28.716 --> 00:02:32.747
For n equals one the sum is just one

52
00:02:32.747 --> 00:02:35.293
and one squared is one.

53
00:02:35.293 --> 00:02:35.930
True.

54
00:02:36.510 --> 00:02:39.777
Step two, the inductive hypothesis.

55
00:02:39.777 --> 00:02:42.203
Take any k, at least one

56
00:02:42.297 --> 00:02:45.470
and assume the first k odd numbers

57
00:02:45.563 --> 00:02:47.430
add up to k squared.

58
00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:52.429
Picture that sum as a k by k square of dots.

59
00:02:52.429 --> 00:02:54.965
We do not know which k.

60
00:02:54.965 --> 00:02:58.980
We only assume P of k for that one k.

61
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.389
Step three, the inductive step.

62
00:03:02.389 --> 00:03:05.948
The next odd number is two k plus one.

63
00:03:05.948 --> 00:03:08.321
Lay it around the square

64
00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:10.876
k dots down the right side

65
00:03:10.876 --> 00:03:14.800
k along the bottom, and one in the corner.

66
00:03:15.150 --> 00:03:19.966
The square is now k plus one by k plus one.

67
00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:24.894
In symbols: k squared, plus two k plus one

68
00:03:24.894 --> 00:03:28.030
equals k plus one, squared.

69
00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:31.066
So P of k plus one is true.

70
00:03:31.165 --> 00:03:34.548
We started from P of k and arrived

71
00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:36.537
at P of k plus one

72
00:03:36.537 --> 00:03:39.820
never in the opposite direction.

73
00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:42.710
Step four, the conclusion.

74
00:03:42.710 --> 00:03:46.176
The base case pushes the first domino

75
00:03:46.176 --> 00:03:47.064
P of one.

76
00:03:47.064 --> 00:03:49.552
The step works for every k

77
00:03:49.552 --> 00:03:52.840
so each domino knocks over the next.

78
00:03:53.190 --> 00:03:56.863
By induction, the sum of the first n odd

79
00:03:56.955 --> 00:03:58.883
numbers is n squared

80
00:03:58.883 --> 00:04:01.270
for every n at least one.

81
00:04:01.850 --> 00:04:03.559
Now a warning.

82
00:04:03.559 --> 00:04:08.321
Claim: n squared plus n is always odd.

83
00:04:08.321 --> 00:04:10.030
Try the step.

84
00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:14.670
k plus one, squared, plus k plus one

85
00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:17.234
is k squared plus k

86
00:04:17.234 --> 00:04:20.530
plus two times k plus one.

87
00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:22.696
Odd plus even is odd.

88
00:04:22.696 --> 00:04:25.809
Every line of that step is correct

89
00:04:25.809 --> 00:04:29.440
so each domino would knock over the next.

90
00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.978
But check the base case.

91
00:04:31.978 --> 00:04:34.895
Zero squared plus zero is zero

92
00:04:34.895 --> 00:04:36.535
and zero is even.

93
00:04:36.535 --> 00:04:39.270
The first domino never falls.

94
00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:43.827
In fact n squared plus n is always even.

95
00:04:43.932 --> 00:04:46.772
A correct step with no base

96
00:04:46.877 --> 00:04:48.980
case proves nothing.

97
00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:54.242
So every proof by induction has four parts.

98
00:04:54.242 --> 00:04:57.945
One, the base case: check P of c.

99
00:04:57.945 --> 00:05:01.865
Two, the hypothesis: assume P of k

100
00:05:01.865 --> 00:05:05.240
for an arbitrary k at least c.

101
00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:08.971
Three, the step: from P of k

102
00:05:08.971 --> 00:05:11.652
prove P of k plus one.

103
00:05:11.652 --> 00:05:13.517
Four, conclude

104
00:05:13.517 --> 00:05:17.830
P of n holds for every n at least c.

105
00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:23.567
Try it yourself: prove that one plus two

106
00:05:23.567 --> 00:05:28.850
up to n, is n times n plus one, over two.

107
00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.682
In part two, the step reaches further back.

108
00:05:32.763 --> 00:05:35.111
Stamps of four cents and five

109
00:05:35.192 --> 00:05:37.459
cents need strong induction

110
00:05:37.459 --> 00:05:39.240
with four base cases.
